course: dictionary
lesson: 7
title: Short forms and abbreviations
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: An abbreviation is a shorter form of a word or phrase. «Mon» is an abbreviation of «Monday», and «km» is a common abbreviation for kilometre. Some abbreviations are just letters that stand for a longer name, like «UK», which stands for United Kingdom.
explain: You will find abbreviations in many places: in maths and science, where units must be written correctly, in signs and forms like «St» for Street, and in texting like «lol». When you meet one you do not know, look at the words around it and ask what longer word would fit.
explain: Scientists have studied whether abbreviations affect how children spell. «Some studies, including research in Britain, found that children who used text abbreviations were not worse at spelling, and some were better; scientists still argue about it.» So a good reader keeps an open mind and checks the evidence.
fact: An abbreviation is a shorter form of a word or phrase.
fact: «Mon» is an abbreviation of «Monday».
fact: «UK» is a set of letters standing for United Kingdom.
fact: «km» stands for kilometre, a unit used in maths and science.
fact: Abbreviation can also mean the act of making something shorter.
exercise: The abbreviation «Mon» is short for which word? | Month | *Monday | Morning | Money
exercise: In maths, «km» is an abbreviation of which unit? | kilogram | *kilometre | kilolitre | kitchen
exercise: Which of these is a set of letters standing for a longer name? | *UK | blue | walk | quick
test: Which abbreviation is correct for «Doctor»? | Doc | Dtr | Dc | *Dr
test: Because texts have no voice or face, what can capitals, emoji and abbreviations do? | Make the message longer | *Show tone, the job a voice and face would do | Change every word's spelling | Stop people reading it
exam: What does the sentence «scientists still argue about it» suggest about abbreviations and spelling? | Only adults use abbreviations | Scientists have proved abbreviations help spelling | Abbreviations always ruin spelling | *Experts do not yet agree on whether abbreviations affect spelling
